{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788269487,"nonce":"F/TIyJ9rM7WfU2x1VBxDHJqg8v8=","message":"PRE-AUTHORIZED so the worker does not stall on it, and so the choice is not made\nsilently. Round that makes blueprints buildable, 2026-09-01.\n\nThis issue offers two shapes and does not pick: a per-verb declared arg set in\n`VerbArgs`, or the narrower \"report which source the value came from\"\n(`pos_source: \"arg\" | \"anchor-default\"`). It is right not to pick from a desk —\nthe cost is only measurable by running the suites — but a worker who has to come\nback and ask has lost the round a serial slot on a one-DLL tree.\n\n\n**Attempt the whitelist. If it breaks a shipped suite for anything other than a\ngenuine caller bug, fall back to the narrow fix and record which suites forced\nit.** Do not come back for permission either way; the fallback is not a failure,\nit is the measurement this issue asked for.\n\n\"A genuine caller bug\" means the suite was passing an argument the verb never\nread — exactly the `at`-instead-of-`pos` class this issue is about. Fix the\nsuite, keep the whitelist. Anything else — an open-ended payload, a verb that\nforwards args to a sub-verb, a documented pass-through — is the whitelist being\ntoo invasive, and one such case is enough to take the fallback for the whole\nchange rather than special-casing it.\n\n\n- `VerbArgs` is in `Source/AutoRimmer/VerbRegistry.cs`, and it already has the\n  right disposition for this: its header states that a value \"present with the\n  wrong type\" is `bad-args` **even for optionals**, because \"the caller is a\n  program, and silently coercing its mistakes hides them\". An unknown KEY is the\n  same argument with the same conclusion. The class is not being bent to a new\n  purpose.\n- There are **119 registered verbs**. That is the size of the declaration\n  burden, and it is the honest reason the narrow fix exists as an option.\n- At least one verb forwards a constructed arg dict to another verb's handler:\n  `StarterKit` builds `{def, count, stuff, quality, pos, stockpile, forbid}` and\n  calls `DevVerbs.SpawnThing(Sub(ctx, args))`. `world-fixture` does the same\n  shape. A whitelist has to be checked at the ENTRY point, not per handler, or\n  these break — and note that `dev:starter-kit` forwarding is exactly where this\n  round is about to ADD a `mode` key (`3a5ff6c`), so the two changes meet.\n\n\nIt is `wave:1` and unrelated to siting, so it looks like a passenger. It is not:\nthis round adds `build {def, pos, rot, stuff}` and `dev:spawn-thing {rot}`, and a\nbuild verb that silently drops `rot` places a real building facing the wrong way,\nmoves its interaction cell with it, and reports success — which is this issue's\nown failure mode with a worse blast radius. Land it after the siting work so a\nsuite break is attributable, but land it.","files":null}]}